              "ImageDescription": {
                "value": "Floppy Disk Drives.\n<ul><li>Qume D/T 8, 8 inch drive, 1.2 MB. This drive was made in 1980. See <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Floppy_Disk_Drive_8_inch.jpg\" title=\"File:Floppy Disk Drive 8 inch.jpg\">Image:Floppy Disk Drive 8 inch.jpg</a></li>\n<li>Tandon TM 100-2A with IBM logo, 5 ¼ inch drive, 360 KB. This drive was made in 1983. See <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IBM_Floppy_Drive_With_DOS.jpg\" title=\"File:IBM Floppy Drive With DOS.jpg\">Image:IBM Floppy Drive With DOS.jpg</a></li>\n<li>Sony MPF920, 3 ½ inch drive, 1.44 MB. This drive was made in 2004.</li></ul>\n<p>This image is used by IBM to illustrate their Icons of Progress: The Floppy Disk article. <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www-03.ibm.com/ibm/history/ibm100/us/en/icons/floppy/transform/\">image 2</a>\n</p>\nTaken with a Canon PowerShot A630 (1/5 second and f/8) under tungsten lighting. The image was touched up with Adobe Photoshop Elements 5.0.",
